From 36d6f76cc976fc6ce9aa4382c6c90f27c49a9e32 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 16:05:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] rs: a recovering or relexing parse of a valid input is linear

Two costs TypeScript does not have made a valid input quadratic in the
Rust engine.

Recovery. With parse.recover.enabled, each update_partial call (fifteen
sites, about six a step) took the value a failed parse would return: a
clone of the value built so far, walked by unwrap_undefined, and held
until the next call, so the next action's append copied the container
it went into. A call now keeps a handle on the chosen node's cell (a
reference count on the cell, not on the value), and Partial::into_value
reads and unwraps the value once, after the parse has failed. The node
is chosen by the same test in the same order and survives recovery's
pops, so the value is the one the last call would have taken; only a
callback that writes the node and then fails before the next call can
tell the two apart, and there the value is the node as the failure left
it, which is what TypeScript returns.

Relex. Every re-cut the lexer rejects ended in an error nobody reads,
and each copied the whole source into full_source three times. The
matchers now build their errors without the source; next_raw_with
attaches it where an error leaves the lexer, and relex calls the
matchers directly, so a rejected cut never pays it. A standing lexer
error is moved into the re-cut's checkpoint rather than copied.

Release build, best of three, old and new interleaved (load 5.6-8.7):
  css      recover  a{b:c} x 2,000 (14 KB)    8.03 s  -> 0.025 s
  css      recover  a{b:c} x 4,000 (28 KB)   62.4 s   -> 0.054 s
  jsonic   recover  [[1],...] 8,000 (32 KB)  44.4 s   -> 0.097 s
  css      relex    a{b:c} x 32,000 (224 KB) 68.0 s   -> 2.54 s
  jsonic   relex    [1,...] 64,000 (128 KB)  66.2 s   -> 4.15 s

rs/tests/linear_time_test.rs pins both (10x the items in under 30x the
time): 10x to 13x now, 71x and 91x on the old code, where it fails.

A differential of the old and new engines over 116,265 jsonic and
80,000 css inputs, mostly invalid, in 19 configurations (recovery and
relex on and off, and recovery narrowed until it gives up) printed the
same values and errors, full_source included, byte for byte; recovery
gave up, and so returned the partial value, in 519,465 of those parses.
doc/per-rule-cost.md records the measurements and the method.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
